Bound the DATA_DIR/configs directory.

The pool maintenance pass writes one "<node_id>.ovpn" per node that is missing
from DATA_DIR/configs and never removes the configs of nodes that left the
pool, so that directory only grows: the container on our arm host had been up
for four weeks and held 22449 files / 264 MB, while nodes.json referenced just
22 of them.

prune_config_dir() runs once at startup and at the end of every maintenance
cycle with the current pool as the keep set. A file is removed only when

  * no node in the pool references it, and
  * it is older than VPNGATE_CONFIG_KEEP_SECONDS (default 24h),

so the config of an in-flight connection is never pulled out from under
openvpn. VPNGATE_CONFIG_MAX_FILES (default 500) is a hard cap on top of that:
if the directory is still larger, the oldest unreferenced files go first.
Setting both knobs to 0 restores the previous unbounded behaviour.

In-flight node tests (".test_<id>_<uuid>.ovpn", created and removed within
seconds) are exempt from the cap and, being far younger than the grace period,
from the age rule as well, so neither rule can cut a probe short; abandoned
test files are collected once they age out. Non-.ovpn files and directories
are never touched, and the number of removed files is printed to stdout (and
therefore mirrored into vpngate.log by the Tee from patch 0001).

diff --git a/vpngate_manager.py b/vpngate_manager.py
